# Two-factor authentication backend TODO

Generated by the light-it auth package. `auth:setup` wrote every 2FA stub,
action and route, but it cannot edit your boilerplate's own login, its
`AppServiceProvider`, or your own `User` model - none of those three files
are generated by this package, so nothing here rewrites them automatically.

## Three manual steps

### 1. The challenge action

Open `LoginAction` and inject the challenge action through its constructor:

```php
{{gateConstructorSnippet}}
```

Then, right after the host login's `$request->session()->regenerate();` and
before `return $user;`, call it:

```php
{{gateCallSnippet}}
```

The challenge action tears the just-created session back down itself before
throwing a challenge, so the call point only matters in that it must run
after `attempt()`/`regenerate()` has committed the session and before the
user is returned - anywhere in that window works.

Every alternative login path this package generates (OTP today; Google SSO
and passkeys as they land) already routes through the same challenge action
via `LoginByUserAction::execute()`. This is the only manual step left to run
your own native login through the same challenge.

Once wired in, a user with 2FA configured gets a `200` with
`token_type: "verification_required"` (or `"setup_required"` if 2FA is
mandatory and they haven't set it up yet) instead of a session on login.
Completing the challenge - the one-time password or a recovery code -
creates the session and returns the same `UserResource` a normal login does.

Each challenge token only completes the endpoint its reason was issued for:
a `setup_required` or `verification_required` token both unlock
`/2fa/complete` - the same token used for `/2fa/setup` works to confirm
enrollment right after - but only a `verification_required` token unlocks
`/2fa/verify-recovery-code`. The token is valid until its TTL expires; that
TTL is `google2fa.challenge_ttl_minutes` in `config/google2fa.php`.

With `google2fa.mandatory=false`, the challenge action only ever challenges a
user who has already set 2FA up (see
`IssueTwoFactorChallengeAction::execute()`); it never hands a
`setup_required` token to a logged-in user who hasn't enrolled yet. Optional,
self-service enrollment from an already-authenticated session is not wired
by this package - it belongs with your account/settings screens.

### 2. The rate limiter

`routes/two-factor-auth.php` already throttles `complete`,
`verify-recovery-code` and `reset` with `throttle:2fa`, but that middleware
needs its named limiter registered before any request hits it. Open
`AppServiceProvider::boot()` and paste this line (anywhere `boot()` runs,
e.g. next to the `login` limiter if you have one):

```php
{{rateLimiterSnippet}}
```

Without this, every request to a `throttle:2fa` route 500s with a missing
rate limiter error instead of being throttled.

### 3. Extend `TwoFactorAuthenticatable`

`\Lightit\Users\Domain\Models\User` must extend
`\Lightit\Authentication\Domain\TwoFactorAuthenticatable` instead of
`Illuminate\Foundation\Auth\User`. Every stub above calls methods
(`hasTwoFactorAuthenticationConfigured()`, `getRecoveryCodes()`, ...) that
only exist on that base class - without this change, every login that
reaches the challenge action throws `BadMethodCallException`.

Delete this file once you've made all three changes.

### Optional: avoid the `Login` event side effect

`$guard->attempt()` fires Laravel's `Login` event even for a user who is about
to be challenged. If your listeners on that event should only run for a fully
authenticated session, swap `attempt()` for `validate()` + the challenge
action + `login()`:

```php
if (! $request->hasSession()) {
    throw new UnauthenticatedException;
}

if (! $guard->validate(['email' => $credentials->email, 'password' => $credentials->password])) {
    throw new UnauthenticatedException;
}

/** @var User $user */
$user = $guard->getLastAttempted();

$this->issueTwoFactorChallengeAction->execute($user);

$guard->login($user);
$request->session()->regenerate();
```

`validate()` checks the credentials without logging the user in, so the
challenge action runs first and the session is only ever established once
the challenge (if any) is cleared - `login()` here does what `attempt()`
used to, and `regenerate()` still runs after it, exactly as it does on the
path this optional snippet replaces.
